"""
# Baremetal `rt_index_of` must be a real definition, not a fabricated weak stub

**Category:** Runtime / SimpleOS freestanding link
**Bug:** doc/08_tracking/bug/text_index_of_on_substring_receiver_reported_bool_2026-08-09.md

## Overview

Cranelift's `is_bare_builtin_collection_method` allowlist routes a bare
(erased-receiver) `.index_of(needle)` to the runtime symbol `rt_index_of`.
That routing is correct — a reloc census on a freestanding probe archive
(`trim().index_of(":")` plus a typed `Str.index_of`) shows exactly one
`rt_index_of` reloc and one `probe__Str_dot_index_of` reloc.

What was missing is the DEFINITION. `examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/
x86_64/boot/baremetal_stubs.c` implemented `rt_array_index_of`, `rt_string_find`
and the sibling `rt_find`, but never `rt_index_of`. The x86_64 freestanding link
also pulls `boot/auto_stubs.c`, which fabricates ~4023 WEAK zero-returning
definitions for any unresolved `rt_*`, so the link succeeded and the guest got a
body whose entire text is `xor %eax,%eax; ret`.

A constant 0 reads as "match at byte offset 0". Every caller guards with
`if idx > 0:`, so it reads as NOT FOUND. Measured in the SimpleOS WM guest,
both values on the SAME receiver in the same run:

    raw_len=15 line_len=15 colon_index_of=0 colon_find_from=11

That one wrong 0 dropped all 45 `:root` CSS custom properties.

## Why a source scan and not a behavioural assertion

The defect only exists in the freestanding link, which cannot be exercised
in-process: `use std.spec` runs on the host runtime, where `rt_index_of` is the
correct `src/runtime/runtime_native.c` definition and passes regardless. The
load-bearing fact is therefore "the arch's stub file defines it", which is
exactly what the fabricated-stub guard could not tell us — `rt_index_of` was
never in `config/simpleos_fabricated_rt_baseline.sdn`, and that guard only
compares against the baseline, so an UNBASELINED fabrication fails OPEN.

Sabotage-checked: deleting the `rt_index_of` body from the stub file turns the
first `it` red, and linking only the weak stub reproduces `xor %eax,%eax; ret`.
"""

use std.spec
use app.io.mod.{file_read}

val X86_64_STUBS = "examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/x86_64/boot/baremetal_stubs.c"

describe "baremetal rt_index_of":
    it "is defined in the x86_64 stub file, not left to the weak fabricator":
        val source = file_read(X86_64_STUBS)
        expect(source).to_contain("RuntimeValue rt_index_of(RuntimeValue recv, RuntimeValue needle)")

    it "keeps its sibling rt_find defined for the same reason":
        val source = file_read(X86_64_STUBS)
        expect(source).to_contain("RuntimeValue rt_find(RuntimeValue recv, RuntimeValue needle)")

    it "returns a RAW index, never the tagged ENCODE_INT rt_array_index_of uses":
        # `rt_index_of`'s contract (hosted reference: runtime_native.c) is a raw
        # byte offset on BOTH branches. Calling the neighbouring
        # `rt_array_index_of` would hand back ENCODE_INT(i) and box a value the
        # caller reads as a plain integer.
        val source = file_read(X86_64_STUBS)
        val body_start = source.index_of("RuntimeValue rt_index_of(RuntimeValue recv")
        expect(body_start > 0).to_be(true)
        val body = source.substring(body_start, body_start + 900)
        expect(body).to_contain("return rt_string_find(recv, needle);")
        expect(body.contains("rt_array_index_of")).to_be(false)
